    bio: "Richard Francis Burton was perhaps the most improbably accomplished human being of the nineteenth century — explorer, linguist, soldier, diplomat, poet, swordsman, and spy, a man who could pass as a native speaker in at least a dozen languages and who spent his life deliberately crossing every boundary, cultural and physical, that Victorian England held sacred. Born in Torquay in 1821 to a restless Anglo-Irish family that moved constantly across Europe, Burton grew up speaking French and Italian before English, a linguistic facility that would define his entire career. He was sent down from Oxford after challenging a fellow student to a duel, and promptly enlisted in the East India Company\'s army, where he acquired Hindustani, Gujarati, Marathi, Sindhi, Punjabi, Telugu, Pashto, and Arabic — not as a party trick but as a tool of survival and intelligence.\n\nHis most audacious feat came in 1853, when Burton disguised himself as a Pathan pilgrim and entered Medina and Mecca — cities forbidden on pain of death to non-Muslims — performing the Hajj in full and filing meticulous notes throughout. Had he been unmasked he would almost certainly have been killed on the spot. He was not unmasked. The account he published on his return, 'Personal Narrative of a Pilgrimage to Al-Madinah and Meccah,' was a sensation that made him famous across Europe. Two years later, again in disguise, he became one of the first Europeans to enter the forbidden city of Harar in present-day Somalia — a journey he undertook largely because he had been told it was impossible.\n\nThe expedition that defined his later reputation was the search for the source of the Nile, which Burton led with John Hanning Speke from 1857 to 1858. The two men reached Lake Tanganyika — the first Europeans to do so — but disease and disagreement poisoned the partnership. Speke went on alone and claimed Lake Victoria as the Nile\'s true source; Burton disputed this vigorously and publicly, and the two men became bitter enemies. The controversy was never resolved between them: Speke died of a gunshot wound — possibly self-inflicted — on the day he was due to debate Burton publicly in 1864. Burton\'s grief and guilt at that outcome, whatever their precise nature, were real and lasting.\n\nBurton\'s other great gift to the world was translation. His unexpurgated English version of 'One Thousand and One Nights,' published in sixteen volumes between 1885 and 1888, brought the full erotic and violent richness of the Arabic original to English readers for the first time, complete with controversial scholarly footnotes that got him accused of obscenity and got the edition banned in several countries. He also translated the Kama Sutra and the Perfumed Garden. When he died in Trieste in 1890, his wife Isabel burned many of his unpublished manuscripts, including a translation of an erotic Arabic work he had spent years completing — an act of destruction that enraged scholars and admirers then, and still does now. He was buried in a stone tent in a Mortlake churchyard, which feels exactly right."
